Database migrations (Alembic)

- Dev/test (SQLite, the default): no action needed - tables are still
  auto-created on first use for a zero-setup experience.
- Production (Postgres): schema is managed here, not by create_all().
  On Render, the Docker image's own startup command runs `alembic upgrade
  head` automatically before starting the server (see Dockerfile's CMD) -
  this exists specifically because Render's "Pre-Deploy Command" feature,
  which would otherwise be the cleaner place for this, is paid-plan only
  (render.yaml here is on the free plan). If this service ever moves to a
  paid, multi-instance plan, switch to a preDeployCommand of
  `alembic upgrade head` in render.yaml and drop it from the Dockerfile's
  CMD instead - running it from every instance's own startup is fine for a
  single instance but not guaranteed-safe if multiple instances start
  concurrently.
  Running it manually elsewhere: `alembic upgrade head`.
- Adopting Alembic on a database that already has tables from before this
  existed (i.e. created by the old create_all()-only path): stamp it at the
  baseline revision first, then upgrade normally:
    alembic stamp fa0f491b5cac   # matches the schema at Stage 1/2
    alembic upgrade head
- After changing src/db/models.py, generate a new migration with:
    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "describe the change"
  then read the generated file before committing it - autogenerate is a
  good first draft, not a guarantee (it won't catch every kind of change,
  e.g. renamed columns look like a drop+add unless you edit it by hand).
